### 4. Non-Adopted Draft Proposals

#### A. SAIL: Source Address Identity List
- **Presenter**: Kevin
- **Slides**: [SAIL Source Address Identity List](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-webbotauth-sail-source-address-identity-list-00)
- **Summary**: Proposal to standardize machine-readable IP egress metadata via well-known URIs using TOML formatting, allowing platforms to declare explicit prefix sets (`exhaustive`, `exclusive`) per service.
- **Discussion**:
  - **Daniel Gillmor** drew parallels between IP-based publishing (SPF) and cryptographic message signing (DKIM), cautioning the group to apply lessons learned from email authentication.
  - **Mark Nottingham**, **Watson Ladd**, and **Srinivasa** agreed that IP publishing formats are currently fragmented across non-standard JSON/HTML pages and that standardized discovery is valuable.
  - **Charter Scope**: **Mark Nottingham** and the Chairs noted that non-cryptographic IP address publishing falls outside the current WebBotAuth charter. AD **Mike Bishop** noted that while retrieving bot metadata is mentioned in the charter, non-cryptographic IP lists might require a charter update or a separate venue.

#### B. Signed Agent Metadata
- **Presenters**: Rim Vogalene, Pablo Gonzalez
- **Slides**: [Signed Agent Metadata](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-webbotauth-signed-agent-metadata-01)
- **Summary**: Proposal to attach granular sub-agent, product, session, or project identifiers to WBA signatures to enable WAFs and origins to perform fine-grained rate limiting and mitigate "shared fate" blocking across multi-tenant AI platforms.
- **Discussion**:
  - **Ted Hardie**, **Daniel Gillmor**, **Martin Thomson**, **Alissa Cooper**, **Eric Rescorla**, and **Samuel Schlesinger** strongly opposed the proposal, emphasizing that sub-agent and user session tracking is explicitly out of scope for WebBotAuth.
  - **Ted Hardie** and **Alissa Cooper** stated that "shared fate" is an intentional security design property intended to force multi-tenant platform operators to police abuse on their own platforms rather than pushing user-level tracking onto origins.
  - **Chair Clarification**: Chair David Schinazi confirmed that fine-grained user and session tracking is out of scope under the current working group charter.

#### C. PACE: Capacity Management for Agents
- **Presenter**: Thibault Meunier
- **Slides**: [PACE Capacity management for agents](https://datatracker.ietf.org/meeting/127/materials/slides-127-webbotauth-pace-capacity-management-for-agents-00)
- **Summary**: Proposal combining WebBotAuth with Masked Oblivious Light (MOL) tokens to enable privacy-preserving, anonymous rate-limiting and quota management for platform agents without exposing user or session identities to origins.
- **Discussion**:
  - **Chris Wood** clarified that PACE was submitted reactively as a privacy-preserving alternative to explicit sub-agent identification proposals.
  - **Eric Rescorla**, **Watson Ladd**, and the Chairs noted that while privacy-preserving token mechanisms are technically superior to raw tracking headers, per-user/per-session capacity management remains out of scope for WebBotAuth.
  - **Chair Guidance**: Chair David Schinazi encouraged participants interested in privacy-preserving tokens and anonymous capacity management to attend the MOL (Masked Oblivious Light) BoF session.

